{"subjectId":"dsa-basics","id":"dictionary","title":"Dictionary","group":"Hashing","order":10,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A dictionary (hash map) stores <strong>key → value</strong> pairs and finds the value for any key in O(1) on average. It is the most used data structure in practice: counting, grouping, caching and indexing are all one hash lookup away.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Dictionary / hash map</strong> — a collection of key–value pairs with <strong>distinct keys</strong>, backed by a hash table: the key is hashed to a slot and the value is stored alongside it. Python <code>dict</code>, C++ <code>unordered_map</code>, Java <code>HashMap</code>, JavaScript <code>Map</code>.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>A hash map is a hash table whose slots hold key–value pairs</figcaption><pre><code>d = {110: 'xyz', 101: 'abc', 105: 'pqr'}     with  h(key) = key % 4\n\nslot 0 │            │\nslot 1 │ 101 → abc  │──▶│ 105 → pqr │     101 % 4 = 1 and 105 % 4 = 1 collide → chained\nslot 2 │ 110 → xyz  │\nslot 3 │            │\n\nlookup d[105]: h(105) = 1 → walk slot 1: 101? no, 105? yes → 'pqr'</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-check\"></i>Properties of a dictionary</h3>\n<div class=\"grid grid--3\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-book\"></i>Key–value pairs</h3><p>Each entry maps a key to exactly one value: <code>d[110] = 'xyz'</code>.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-key\"></i>Distinct keys</h3><p>Assigning to an existing key <strong>replaces</strong> its value; there are never two entries with the same key.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-copy\"></i>Values may repeat</h3><p>Many keys can map to the same value — only keys are unique.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-shuffle\"></i>Unordered (conceptually)</h3><p>Entries live in hash slots. Python 3.7+ and JavaScript additionally remember insertion order; C++ and Java do not.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bolt\"></i>Hashing internally</h3><p>Every lookup hashes the key — O(1) average, O(n) worst case.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock\"></i>Hashable keys</h3><p>Keys must be immutable with a stable hash (numbers, strings, tuples). Values can be anything.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Python</th><th>Average</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>insert / update</td><td><code>d[k] = v</code></td><td>O(1)</td><td>Hash <code>k</code>; store or overwrite in its slot</td></tr><tr><td>lookup</td><td><code>d[k]</code> (KeyError if missing) / <code>d.get(k, default)</code></td><td>O(1)</td><td>Hash <code>k</code>; compare keys in that one slot</td></tr><tr><td>membership</td><td><code>k in d</code></td><td>O(1)</td><td>Same lookup, returns a boolean</td></tr><tr><td>delete</td><td><code>del d[k]</code> / <code>d.pop(k)</code></td><td>O(1)</td><td>Hash <code>k</code>; unlink the entry</td></tr><tr><td>size</td><td><code>len(d)</code></td><td>O(1)</td><td>A stored counter</td></tr><tr><td>iterate</td><td><code>for k, v in d.items()</code></td><td>O(n)</td><td>Walk every slot</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-box\"></i>Map containers in the four languages</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Language</th><th>Hash map (unordered, O(1) avg)</th><th>Ordered map (sorted keys, O(log n))</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Python</td><td><code>dict</code> (insertion-ordered since 3.7); <code>collections.Counter</code>, <code>defaultdict</code></td><td>— (use <code>sorted(d)</code>; <code>sortedcontainers</code> third-party)</td></tr><tr><td>C++</td><td><code>std::unordered_map</code></td><td><code>std::map</code> (red-black tree)</td></tr><tr><td>Java</td><td><code>HashMap</code>; <code>LinkedHashMap</code> keeps insertion order</td><td><code>TreeMap</code> (red-black tree)</td></tr><tr><td>JavaScript</td><td><code>Map</code> (insertion-ordered); plain objects <code>{}</code> for string keys only</td><td>— (sort the keys)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>JavaScript objects <code>{}</code> convert every key to a string (<code>obj[1]</code> and <code>obj[\"1\"]</code> are the same key). Use <code>Map</code> when keys are numbers, objects or mixed types, and whenever you need <code>size</code> without <code>Object.keys(...).length</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-plus\"></i>Creating a dictionary, adding and reading entries</h3>\n<p>A dictionary is created from a literal or built up one assignment at a time. Reading <code>d[key]</code> returns the value; <code>keys()</code>, <code>values()</code> and <code>items()</code> give the three views. Below, the keys are <strong>sorted</strong> before printing because iteration order differs between languages.</p>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>d = {}                       # same as {110: 'xyz', 101: 'abc', 105: 'pqr'}\nd[110] = 'xyz'\nd[101] = 'abc'\nd[105] = 'pqr'\nprint(\"size:\", len(d))\nprint(\"d[101] =\", d[101])                        # O(1) lookup\nprint(\"keys:\", *sorted(d.keys()))\nprint(\"values:\", *[d[k] for k in sorted(d)])\nprint(\"items:\", *[f\"{k}={d[k]}\" for k in sorted(d)])\nd[101] = 'ABC'                                   # existing key → value replaced\nprint(\"after d[101] = 'ABC':\", *[f\"{k}={d[k]}\" for k in sorted(d)])</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;unordered_map&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nvector&lt;int&gt; sortedKeys(const unordered_map&lt;int, string&gt;&amp; d) {\n    vector&lt;int&gt; keys;\n    for (auto&amp; kv : d) keys.push_back(kv.first);\n    sort(keys.begin(), keys.end());\n    return keys;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    unordered_map&lt;int, string&gt; d;    // same as {{110, \"xyz\"}, {101, \"abc\"}, {105, \"pqr\"}}\n    d[110] = \"xyz\";\n    d[101] = \"abc\";\n    d[105] = \"pqr\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"size: \" &lt;&lt; d.size() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"d[101] = \" &lt;&lt; d[101] &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";                 // O(1) lookup\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"keys:\"; for (int k : sortedKeys(d)) cout &lt;&lt; \" \" &lt;&lt; k; cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"values:\"; for (int k : sortedKeys(d)) cout &lt;&lt; \" \" &lt;&lt; d[k]; cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"items:\"; for (int k : sortedKeys(d)) cout &lt;&lt; \" \" &lt;&lt; k &lt;&lt; \"=\" &lt;&lt; d[k]; cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    d[101] = \"ABC\";                                          // existing key → value replaced\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"after d[101] = 'ABC':\"; for (int k : sortedKeys(d)) cout &lt;&lt; \" \" &lt;&lt; k &lt;&lt; \"=\" &lt;&lt; d[k]; cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static String items(Map&lt;Integer, String&gt; d) {\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (int k : new TreeSet&lt;&gt;(d.keySet())) sb.append(\" \").append(k).append(\"=\").append(d.get(k));\n        return sb.toString();\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Map&lt;Integer, String&gt; d = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();   // same as Map.of(110, \"xyz\", 101, \"abc\", 105, \"pqr\")\n        d.put(110, \"xyz\");\n        d.put(101, \"abc\");\n        d.put(105, \"pqr\");\n        System.out.println(\"size: \" + d.size());\n        System.out.println(\"d[101] = \" + d.get(101));          // O(1) lookup\n        TreeSet&lt;Integer&gt; keys = new TreeSet&lt;&gt;(d.keySet());\n        StringBuilder ks = new StringBuilder(\"keys:\"), vs = new StringBuilder(\"values:\");\n        for (int k : keys) { ks.append(\" \").append(k); vs.append(\" \").append(d.get(k)); }\n        System.out.println(ks);\n        System.out.println(vs);\n        System.out.println(\"items:\" + items(d));\n        d.put(101, \"ABC\");                                      // existing key → value replaced\n        System.out.println(\"after d[101] = 'ABC':\" + items(d));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const d = new Map();          // same as new Map([[110, 'xyz'], [101, 'abc'], [105, 'pqr']])\nd.set(110, 'xyz');\nd.set(101, 'abc');\nd.set(105, 'pqr');\nconst sortedKeys = () =&gt; [...d.keys()].sort((a, b) =&gt; a - b);\nconst items = () =&gt; sortedKeys().map(k =&gt; `${k}=${d.get(k)}`).join(\" \");\n\nconsole.log(\"size:\", d.size);\nconsole.log(\"d[101] =\", d.get(101));                 // O(1) lookup\nconsole.log(\"keys:\", sortedKeys().join(\" \"));\nconsole.log(\"values:\", sortedKeys().map(k =&gt; d.get(k)).join(\" \"));\nconsole.log(\"items:\", items());\nd.set(101, 'ABC');                                    // existing key → value replaced\nconsole.log(\"after d[101] = 'ABC':\", items());</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>size: 3\nd[101] = abc\nkeys: 101 105 110\nvalues: abc pqr xyz\nitems: 101=abc 105=pqr 110=xyz\nafter d[101] = 'ABC': 101=ABC 105=pqr 110=xyz</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(1) average per insert / lookup</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n)</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>In C++ <code>d[key]</code> on a <strong>missing</strong> key silently <em>inserts</em> a default value (empty string, 0) and returns it. Use <code>d.find(key)</code>, <code>d.count(key)</code>, <code>d.contains(key)</code> (C++20) or <code>d.at(key)</code> (throws) when you only want to read.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-help-circle\"></i>Missing keys and default values</h3>\n<p>What happens when the key is absent is where the languages differ most, and the source of most dictionary bugs:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Language</th><th>Read of a missing key</th><th>Safe read with default</th><th>Membership</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Python</td><td><code>d[k]</code> raises <code>KeyError</code></td><td><code>d.get(k, default)</code>; <code>d.setdefault(k, default)</code> also inserts</td><td><code>k in d</code></td></tr><tr><td>C++</td><td><code>d[k]</code> <strong>inserts</strong> a default and returns it; <code>d.at(k)</code> throws</td><td><code>auto it = d.find(k); it != d.end() ? it-&gt;second : default</code></td><td><code>d.count(k)</code> / <code>d.contains(k)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Java</td><td><code>d.get(k)</code> returns <code>null</code></td><td><code>d.getOrDefault(k, default)</code></td><td><code>d.containsKey(k)</code></td></tr><tr><td>JavaScript</td><td><code>d.get(k)</code> returns <code>undefined</code></td><td><code>d.get(k) ?? default</code></td><td><code>d.has(k)</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>d = {110: 'xyz', 101: 'abc', 105: 'pqr'}\nprint(\"get 101:\", d.get(101))\nprint(\"get 999:\", d.get(999, \"none\"))           # no KeyError\nprint(\"999 in d:\", \"true\" if 999 in d else \"false\")\n\ncounts = {}\ncounts['abc'] = counts.get('abc', 0) + 1       # the counting idiom\nprint(\"count of 'abc' with default 0 →\", counts['abc'])</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;unordered_map&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nstring getOr(const unordered_map&lt;int, string&gt;&amp; d, int k, const string&amp; def) {\n    auto it = d.find(k);                          // find does not insert\n    return it != d.end() ? it-&gt;second : def;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    unordered_map&lt;int, string&gt; d = {{110, \"xyz\"}, {101, \"abc\"}, {105, \"pqr\"}};\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"get 101: \" &lt;&lt; getOr(d, 101, \"none\") &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"get 999: \" &lt;&lt; getOr(d, 999, \"none\") &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"999 in d: \" &lt;&lt; (d.contains(999) ? \"true\" : \"false\") &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n\n    unordered_map&lt;string, int&gt; counts;\n    counts[\"abc\"]++;                              // operator[] inserts 0 then increments — the counting idiom\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"count of 'abc' with default 0 → \" &lt;&lt; counts[\"abc\"] &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Map&lt;Integer, String&gt; d = new HashMap&lt;&gt;(Map.of(110, \"xyz\", 101, \"abc\", 105, \"pqr\"));\n        System.out.println(\"get 101: \" + d.get(101));\n        System.out.println(\"get 999: \" + d.getOrDefault(999, \"none\"));   // get(999) alone would be null\n        System.out.println(\"999 in d: \" + d.containsKey(999));\n\n        Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; counts = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();\n        counts.merge(\"abc\", 1, Integer::sum);           // the counting idiom\n        System.out.println(\"count of 'abc' with default 0 → \" + counts.get(\"abc\"));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const d = new Map([[110, 'xyz'], [101, 'abc'], [105, 'pqr']]);\nconsole.log(\"get 101:\", d.get(101));\nconsole.log(\"get 999:\", d.get(999) ?? \"none\");        // get(999) alone would be undefined\nconsole.log(\"999 in d:\", d.has(999));\n\nconst counts = new Map();\ncounts.set('abc', (counts.get('abc') ?? 0) + 1);     // the counting idiom\nconsole.log(\"count of 'abc' with default 0 →\", counts.get('abc'));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>get 101: abc\nget 999: none\n999 in d: false\ncount of 'abc' with default 0 → 1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(1) average</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Python's <code>collections.defaultdict(int)</code> and <code>Counter</code> build the default-value idiom into the container: <code>counts[x] += 1</code> just works. <code>Counter(arr).most_common(k)</code> is the one-liner for top-k frequency questions.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-trash\"></i>Removing entries</h3>\n<p><code>pop(key)</code> removes an entry <strong>and returns its value</strong> (Python, and the same shape in Java's <code>remove</code>); <code>del d[key]</code> just removes it. Python's <code>popitem()</code> removes and returns the <strong>last inserted</strong> pair — handy as a stack of pairs — and has no equivalent in the other languages.</p>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>d = {110: 'xyz', 101: 'abc', 105: 'pqr'}\ndef show(): print(\"remaining:\", *[f\"{k}={d[k]}\" for k in sorted(d)])\n\nprint(\"pop 101 →\", d.pop(101))             # removes and returns the value\nshow()\nprint(\"delete 105:\", \"true\" if d.pop(105, None) is not None else \"false\")\nprint(\"delete 999:\", \"true\" if d.pop(999, None) is not None else \"false\")   # default avoids KeyError\nshow()</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;unordered_map&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nunordered_map&lt;int, string&gt; d = {{110, \"xyz\"}, {101, \"abc\"}, {105, \"pqr\"}};\n\nvoid show() {\n    vector&lt;int&gt; keys;\n    for (auto&amp; kv : d) keys.push_back(kv.first);\n    sort(keys.begin(), keys.end());\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"remaining:\";\n    for (int k : keys) cout &lt;&lt; \" \" &lt;&lt; k &lt;&lt; \"=\" &lt;&lt; d[k];\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}\n\nint main() {\n    string v = d.at(101); d.erase(101);        // read, then erase — erase itself returns a count\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"pop 101 → \" &lt;&lt; v &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    show();\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"delete 105: \" &lt;&lt; (d.erase(105) ? \"true\" : \"false\") &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"delete 999: \" &lt;&lt; (d.erase(999) ? \"true\" : \"false\") &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    show();\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static Map&lt;Integer, String&gt; d = new HashMap&lt;&gt;(Map.of(110, \"xyz\", 101, \"abc\", 105, \"pqr\"));\n\n    static void show() {\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(\"remaining:\");\n        for (int k : new TreeSet&lt;&gt;(d.keySet())) sb.append(\" \").append(k).append(\"=\").append(d.get(k));\n        System.out.println(sb);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"pop 101 → \" + d.remove(101));      // removes and returns the value (null if absent)\n        show();\n        System.out.println(\"delete 105: \" + (d.remove(105) != null));\n        System.out.println(\"delete 999: \" + (d.remove(999) != null));\n        show();\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const d = new Map([[110, 'xyz'], [101, 'abc'], [105, 'pqr']]);\nconst show = () =&gt; console.log(\"remaining:\", [...d.keys()].sort((a, b) =&gt; a - b).map(k =&gt; `${k}=${d.get(k)}`).join(\" \"));\n\nconst v = d.get(101); d.delete(101);        // read, then delete — delete returns a boolean\nconsole.log(\"pop 101 →\", v);\nshow();\nconsole.log(\"delete 105:\", d.delete(105));\nconsole.log(\"delete 999:\", d.delete(999));\nshow();</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>pop 101 → abc\nremaining: 105=pqr 110=xyz\ndelete 105: true\ndelete 999: false\nremaining: 110=xyz</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(1) average per removal</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(1)</span></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>d = {110: 'xyz', 101: 'abc', 105: 'pqr'}\nprint(d.popitem())      # last inserted pair, LIFO (Python 3.7+)\nprint(d.popitem())\nprint(d)</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>(105, 'pqr')\n(101, 'abc')\n{110: 'xyz'}</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-sort\"></i>Iteration order</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Conceptually a hash map has <strong>no order</strong>. Two of the four languages guarantee insertion order anyway, two do not — code that works in Python and breaks in Java or C++ is a classic porting bug.</p></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Container</th><th>Iteration order</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Python <code>dict</code></td><td><strong>Insertion order</strong> (guaranteed since 3.7)</td></tr><tr><td>C++ <code>unordered_map</code></td><td>Arbitrary; <code>std::map</code> = sorted by key</td></tr><tr><td>Java <code>HashMap</code></td><td>Arbitrary; <code>LinkedHashMap</code> = insertion order; <code>TreeMap</code> = sorted by key</td></tr><tr><td>JavaScript <code>Map</code></td><td><strong>Insertion order</strong>; plain objects list integer-like keys first in ascending order, then string keys in insertion order</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Need sorted output? Sort the keys explicitly (<code>for k in sorted(d)</code>) or use a tree map. Need first-seen order in C++/Java? Keep a separate vector/list of keys in insertion order or use <code>LinkedHashMap</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-apps\"></i>Typical uses</h3>\n<div class=\"grid grid--3\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-chart-bar\"></i>Frequency counting</h3><p><code>count[x] += 1</code> over an array or text — then answer <em>most frequent</em>, <em>appears exactly once</em>, <em>anagram check</em>.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-book\"></i>Lookup tables</h3><p>id → record, name → phone number, word → definition. A database index in miniature.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bolt\"></i>Memoisation / caching</h3><p>key = function arguments, value = result; dynamic programming top-down uses exactly this.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-category\"></i>Grouping</h3><p>key = group label (first letter, sorted letters, remainder), value = list of members.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-shuffle\"></i>Pair problems</h3><p>Two-sum: for each <code>x</code>, has <code>target - x</code> been seen? Store value → index.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-topology-star\"></i>Graphs</h3><p>Adjacency lists for sparse graphs with arbitrary vertex labels: <code>adj[u] = [v, …]</code>.</p></div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-chart-bar\"></i>Problem: word frequencies</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>text = \"the cat and the hat and the bat\"</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>and 2 / bat 1 / cat 1 / hat 1 / the 3</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Split into words and do <code>freq[word] += 1</code> for each — a hash map update is O(1) on average, so the whole text costs O(n). Print the keys in sorted order so the result does not depend on the language's iteration order.</p></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def word_freq(text):\n    freq = {}\n    for w in text.split():\n        freq[w] = freq.get(w, 0) + 1      # O(1) average\n    for w in sorted(freq):\n        print(w, freq[w])\n\nword_freq(\"the cat and the hat and the bat\")</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;sstream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;unordered_map&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nvoid wordFreq(const string&amp; text) {\n    unordered_map&lt;string, int&gt; freq;\n    istringstream in(text);\n    string w;\n    while (in &gt;&gt; w) freq[w]++;            // O(1) average\n    vector&lt;string&gt; words;\n    for (auto&amp; kv : freq) words.push_back(kv.first);\n    sort(words.begin(), words.end());\n    for (const string&amp; x : words) cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; \" \" &lt;&lt; freq[x] &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}\n\nint main() {\n    wordFreq(\"the cat and the hat and the bat\");\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static void wordFreq(String text) {\n        Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; freq = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();\n        for (String w : text.split(\" \")) freq.merge(w, 1, Integer::sum);   // O(1) average\n        for (String w : new TreeSet&lt;&gt;(freq.keySet()))\n            System.out.println(w + \" \" + freq.get(w));\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        wordFreq(\"the cat and the hat and the bat\");\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function wordFreq(text) {\n    const freq = new Map();\n    for (const w of text.split(\" \")) freq.set(w, (freq.get(w) ?? 0) + 1);   // O(1) average\n    for (const w of [...freq.keys()].sort()) console.log(w, freq.get(w));\n}\n\nwordFreq(\"the cat and the hat and the bat\");</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>and 2\nbat 1\ncat 1\nhat 1\nthe 3</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n) average (+ O(k log k) to sort k distinct words)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(k)</span></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-shuffle\"></i>Problem: two sum with a hash map</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>arr = [8, 3, 5, 11, 2], target = 13</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>8 + 5 = 13</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>arr = [1, 2, 3], target = 10</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>no pair</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Scan once. For each <code>x</code>, the partner we need is <code>target - x</code>; if it has already been <strong>seen</strong>, we are done, otherwise record <code>x</code>. One hash lookup per element replaces the inner loop of the O(n²) brute force.</p></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Create an empty map</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>seen</code> maps a value to its index (a set suffices if indices are not needed).</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">For each element <code>x</code></div><div class=\"step-desc\">Compute <code>need = target - x</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Check the map</div><div class=\"step-desc\">If <code>need</code> is in <code>seen</code>, report the pair <code>(need, x)</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">4</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Record</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Otherwise store <code>seen[x] = index</code> and continue.</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def two_sum(arr, target):\n    seen = {}                               # value → index\n    for i, x in enumerate(arr):\n        need = target - x\n        if need in seen:                    # O(1) instead of an inner loop\n            return seen[need], i\n        seen[x] = i\n    return None\n\nfor arr, target in (([8, 3, 5, 11, 2], 13), ([1, 2, 3], 10)):\n    res = two_sum(arr, target)\n    label = f\"[{' '.join(map(str, arr))}] target {target} →\"\n    if res is None:\n        print(label, \"no pair\")\n    else:\n        i, j = res\n        print(label, f\"{arr[i]} + {arr[j]} = {target} (indices {i}, {j})\")</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n#include &lt;unordered_map&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nbool twoSum(const vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; arr, int target, int&amp; i, int&amp; j) {\n    unordered_map&lt;int, int&gt; seen;           // value → index\n    int n = arr.size();\n    for (int k = 0; k &lt; n; k++) {\n        int need = target - arr[k];\n        auto it = seen.find(need);          // O(1) instead of an inner loop\n        if (it != seen.end()) { i = it-&gt;second; j = k; return true; }\n        seen[arr[k]] = k;\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    vector&lt;pair&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;, int&gt;&gt; tests = {{{8, 3, 5, 11, 2}, 13}, {{1, 2, 3}, 10}};\n    for (auto&amp; [arr, target] : tests) {\n        cout &lt;&lt; \"[\";\n        for (int k = 0; k &lt; (int)arr.size(); k++) cout &lt;&lt; (k ? \" \" : \"\") &lt;&lt; arr[k];\n        cout &lt;&lt; \"] target \" &lt;&lt; target &lt;&lt; \" → \";\n        int i, j;\n        if (twoSum(arr, target, i, j))\n            cout &lt;&lt; arr[i] &lt;&lt; \" + \" &lt;&lt; arr[j] &lt;&lt; \" = \" &lt;&lt; target &lt;&lt; \" (indices \" &lt;&lt; i &lt;&lt; \", \" &lt;&lt; j &lt;&lt; \")\\n\";\n        else\n            cout &lt;&lt; \"no pair\\n\";\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static int[] twoSum(int[] arr, int target) {\n        Map&lt;Integer, Integer&gt; seen = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();   // value → index\n        for (int k = 0; k &lt; arr.length; k++) {\n            int need = target - arr[k];\n            if (seen.containsKey(need))                 // O(1) instead of an inner loop\n                return new int[]{seen.get(need), k};\n            seen.put(arr[k], k);\n        }\n        return null;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[][] arrays = {{8, 3, 5, 11, 2}, {1, 2, 3}};\n        int[] targets = {13, 10};\n        for (int t = 0; t &lt; arrays.length; t++) {\n            int[] arr = arrays[t];\n            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(\"[\");\n            for (int k = 0; k &lt; arr.length; k++) sb.append(k &gt; 0 ? \" \" : \"\").append(arr[k]);\n            sb.append(\"] target \").append(targets[t]).append(\" → \");\n            int[] res = twoSum(arr, targets[t]);\n            if (res == null) sb.append(\"no pair\");\n            else sb.append(arr[res[0]]).append(\" + \").append(arr[res[1]]).append(\" = \").append(targets[t])\n                   .append(\" (indices \").append(res[0]).append(\", \").append(res[1]).append(\")\");\n            System.out.println(sb);\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function twoSum(arr, target) {\n    const seen = new Map();                   // value → index\n    for (let k = 0; k &lt; arr.length; k++) {\n        const need = target - arr[k];\n        if (seen.has(need)) return [seen.get(need), k];   // O(1) instead of an inner loop\n        seen.set(arr[k], k);\n    }\n    return null;\n}\n\nfor (const [arr, target] of [[[8, 3, 5, 11, 2], 13], [[1, 2, 3], 10]]) {\n    const label = `[${arr.join(\" \")}] target ${target} →`;\n    const res = twoSum(arr, target);\n    if (res === null) console.log(label, \"no pair\");\n    else console.log(label, `${arr[res[0]]} + ${arr[res[1]]} = ${target} (indices ${res[0]}, ${res[1]})`);\n}</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[8 3 5 11 2] target 13 → 8 + 5 = 13 (indices 0, 2)\n[1 2 3] target 10 → no pair</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n) average</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n)</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>Insert <code>x</code> into the map <strong>after</strong> checking for its partner, not before — otherwise <code>target = 2x</code> would match an element with itself (<code>[5]</code>, target 10 → \"5 + 5\").</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-message-question\"></i>Interview Q&amp;A</h3>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How does a dictionary achieve O(1) lookup?</summary><div><p>The key is hashed to an array index; the value sits in that slot (or in a short chain / probe sequence starting there). No scanning, no comparisons with unrelated keys. The cost is O(1) <em>average</em>: a bad hash function or an overfull table lengthens chains, and rehashing keeps the load factor bounded.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Dictionary vs set — when do you need which?</summary><div><p>Both are hash tables with distinct keys. A set answers <em>is <code>x</code> present?</em>; a dictionary additionally attaches a value to each key — a count, an index, a list of members. If you catch yourself using a dictionary with all values <code>True</code>, you want a set.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What can be a dictionary key?</summary><div><p>Anything hashable and immutable in Python: numbers, strings, tuples of hashables, <code>frozenset</code> — not lists, dicts or sets. Java and C++ require a consistent <code>hashCode</code>/<code>equals</code> pair (or <code>std::hash</code> plus <code>==</code>) and the object must not change while it is a key. JavaScript <code>Map</code> accepts any value including objects (compared by identity), whereas plain objects stringify every key.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between HashMap, LinkedHashMap and TreeMap (or dict vs std::map)?</summary><div><p><code>HashMap</code>/<code>unordered_map</code>/<code>dict</code>: hash table, O(1) average, no ordering guarantee (Python <code>dict</code> and JS <code>Map</code> keep insertion order). <code>LinkedHashMap</code>: hash table plus a linked list through the entries — insertion order at the same O(1). <code>TreeMap</code>/<code>std::map</code>: red-black tree, keys sorted, O(log n) per operation, supports floor/ceiling/range queries. Pick by whether you need order.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How would you find the first non-repeating character in a string?</summary><div><p>Two passes with a dictionary: count every character (O(n)), then scan the string again and return the first character whose count is 1. The second pass uses the <em>string's</em> order, so the hash map's iteration order never matters.</p></div></details></section>"}